==Latin==
===Etymology===
{{wikipedia|Britain (name)}}
As ''Brittānia'' from the 1st century BC, from Greek Πρεττανια (Diodorus), earlier [νῆσος] Πρεττανική or Βρεττανίαι, used by Pytheas (4th century BC) of the entire archipelago now known as the [[British Isles]].

The Greek name is ultimately from a Celtic ethnonym, reconstructed as  early British Celtic (P-Celtic) *''Pritani'', perhaps from a {{proto|Celtic}} *''kʷritanī'', *''kʷritenī'', whence  {{etyl|cy|-}} {{term|Prydyn}} 'Picts', {{etyl|sga|-}} ''Cruthne, Cru(i)then-túath'' 'Picts'.

